  "summary": "In Luke and Acts, the synagogue is a local gathering place where Scripture is read, taught, and discussed. These scenes show why synagogues matter to Jesus’ ministry and Paul’s mission, while leaving their earlier origins outside what this record’s cited passages can establish.",
  "historical_context": "Luke 4:16–21 depicts Jesus reading Isaiah and explaining its relevance in a synagogue; Acts 13:14–16 and 17:1–4 portray Paul speaking and reasoning in synagogue settings. These texts illuminate synagogue life as the New Testament presents it, but they do not explain when synagogues began or establish that exile created them.",
  "ancient_near_east_context": "",
  "hebraic_worldview": "",
  "second_temple_context": "",
  "canonical_context": "Deuteronomy 6:4–9 commands Israel to keep the words of the covenant in daily household teaching; it does not describe synagogue institutions. Psalm 74:8–9 laments the burning of places of assembly and the absence of prophetic signs, but does not identify those places as synagogues. Luke and Acts provide the clearest synagogue scenes among this entry’s references.",
